What is OOP?

Object-Oriented Programming (OOP) is a programming paradigm that organizes software around objects rather than functions.

An object contains:

Data (Attributes)
Behavior (Methods)
Advantages
Code Reusability
Easy Maintenance
Security
Flexibility
Scalability
Real-world Modeling
Class

A Class is a blueprint or template used to create objects.

Example
class Student{
    int id;
    String name;

    void display(){
        System.out.println(id + " " + name);
    }
}
Object

An Object is an instance of a class.

Example
Student s1 = new Student();

s1.id = 101;
s1.name = "Anekka";

s1.display();
Constructor

A constructor is a special method that is automatically called when an object is created.

Characteristics:

Same name as class
No return type
Initializes object
Default Constructor
class Student{

    Student(){
        System.out.println("Object Created");
    }
}
Parameterized Constructor
class Student{

    String name;

    Student(String name){
        this.name = name;
    }
}
this Keyword

this refers to the current object.

Example
class Student{

    int id;

    Student(int id){
        this.id = id;
    }
}
Inheritance

Inheritance allows one class to acquire properties and methods of another class.

Uses the extends keyword.

Example
class Animal{

    void sound(){
        System.out.println("Animal Sound");
    }
}

class Dog extends Animal{

    void bark(){
        System.out.println("Bark");
    }
}
Types of Inheritance
Single Inheritance
Multilevel Inheritance
Hierarchical Inheritance

(Java does not support Multiple Inheritance through classes.)

super Keyword

Used to access parent class methods and constructors.

Example
class Animal{

    Animal(){
        System.out.println("Animal Constructor");
    }
}

class Dog extends Animal{

    Dog(){
        super();
        System.out.println("Dog Constructor");
    }
}
Polymorphism

Polymorphism means one interface, many forms.

Two Types:

Compile-time Polymorphism
Runtime Polymorphism
Method Overloading (Compile-Time)

Same method name but different parameters.

Example
class Calculator{

    int add(int a,int b){
        return a+b;
    }

    int add(int a,int b,int c){
        return a+b+c;
    }
}
Method Overriding (Runtime)

Child class provides its own implementation of the parent class method.

Example
class Animal{

    void sound(){
        System.out.println("Animal");
    }
}

class Dog extends Animal{

    @Override
    void sound(){
        System.out.println("Bark");
    }
}
Encapsulation

Encapsulation means wrapping data and methods into a single unit.

Use private variables and public getters/setters.

Example
class Employee{

    private int salary;

    public void setSalary(int salary){
        this.salary = salary;
    }

    public int getSalary(){
        return salary;
    }
}
Advantages
Data Hiding
Better Security
Easy Maintenance
Abstraction

Abstraction hides implementation details and shows only essential functionality.

Can be achieved using:

Abstract Class
Interface
Abstract Class
abstract class Animal{

    abstract void sound();
}

class Dog extends Animal{

    void sound(){
        System.out.println("Bark");
    }
}
Interface

An interface contains only abstract methods (before Java 8).

Example
interface Vehicle{

    void start();
}

class Car implements Vehicle{

    public void start(){
        System.out.println("Car Started");
    }
}
final Keyword

The final keyword is used to restrict modification.

Final Variable

Cannot change value.

final int age = 20;
Final Method

Cannot be overridden.

final void display(){

}
Final Class

Cannot be inherited.

final class Student{

}
static Keyword

Belongs to the class instead of objects.

Example
class Student{

    static String college = "RMD";

    String name;
}
Object Class Methods

Some commonly used methods:

toString()

equals()

hashCode()

clone()
Java Annotations

Annotations provide metadata about Java code.

They help the compiler and frameworks understand how the code should behave.

1. @Override

Indicates that a method overrides a parent class method.

@Override
public void display(){

}
Benefit
Compiler checks whether overriding is correct.
Prevents spelling mistakes.
2. @Deprecated

Marks a method or class as outdated.

@Deprecated
public void oldMethod(){

}

Compiler shows a warning when it is used.

3. @SuppressWarnings

Suppresses compiler warnings.

@SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
4. @FunctionalInterface

Indicates an interface contains only one abstract method.

@FunctionalInterface
interface Demo{

    void display();
}

Used in Lambda Expressions.

5. @SafeVarargs

Suppresses warnings related to variable arguments.

@SafeVarargs

OOP Pillars Summary
Concept	Description
Encapsulation	Hides data using private variables
Inheritance	Reuses code from parent class
Polymorphism	One method behaves differently
Abstraction	Hides implementation details
Difference Between Overloading and Overriding
Method Overloading	Method Overriding
Same method name	Same method name
Different parameters	Same parameters
Same class	Parent & Child classes
Compile-time	Runtime
Increases readability	Enables runtime polymorphism
Difference Between Abstract Class and Interface
Abstract Class	Interface
Uses abstract keyword	Uses interface keyword
Can have constructors	Cannot have constructors
Can have normal methods	Mainly abstract methods (plus default/static methods in modern Java)
Supports partial abstraction	Supports abstraction
Difference Between Class and Object
Class	Object
Blueprint	Instance of a class
No memory allocated	Memory allocated
Logical entity	Physical entity
Day Summary
OOP organizes programs using objects and classes.
A class is a blueprint, while an object is its instance.
Constructors initialize objects.
Inheritance enables code reuse.
Encapsulation hides data using private fields.
Polymorphism allows methods to have multiple forms through overloading and overriding.
Abstraction hides implementation details using abstract classes and interfaces.
The this, super, static, and final keywords provide important object-oriented features.
Java annotations such as @Override, @Deprecated, and @FunctionalInterface improve code quality and readability.
